      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 133,
      "outputTokens": 1016,
      "costUsd": 0.0092105,
      "pronunciationText": "Here's what a zed score does to the whole curve. Subtracting the mean slides the bell so its centre sits at zero. Dividing by sigma squeezes it until its standard deviation is one. Do that to both bells, and they land on the same curve. It's the standard normal distribution, with mean zero and standard deviation one, written capital Z is N of zero, one. Any other normal, with mean mu and standard deviation sigma, is a non-standard normal. The value marks move with the curve, so the areas don't change. The area beyond forty-five centimetres is the area beyond one point two five."
    },
